    progression: ["Add a small load when all sets hit the top of the range with the same technique.", "During a heavy running week, hold the loads and drop one set rather than pushing an increase.", "In the last ten days before a race, keep the movements but cut volume roughly in half and remove the hopping.", "If a run feels flat two sessions in a row, the lifting is placed wrong or is too close to failure."],
    recovery: ["Expect soreness for the first two weeks and then far less; that early soreness is not a reason to abandon the plan.", "Do not add lifting volume in the same week you add mileage. Change one variable at a time.", "Calf and foot complaints usually mean the ankle work was skipped, not that lifting is the problem."],
    faq: [{ question: "Will lifting make me heavier and slower?", answer: "Two sessions a week at these volumes build force and tendon capacity long before they add meaningful size. Most runners get faster over a season, not slower." }, { question: "Should I lift before or after a run?", answer: "After, on a day that was already hard. Lifting first compromises the run, and putting it on an easy day removes the easy day." }, { question: "What about during race week?", answer: "Keep one short session early in the week at half the usual volume so the pattern stays familiar, and drop the jumping." }],
    safety: "Stop if an exercise produces sharp pain, dizziness, or chest pain. Bone stress injuries need a clinician rather than a lighter set, and pain that worsens across a week is not something to train through.",
